  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2032}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{\u2032}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{\u2032}(t) \\\\ t\\,i+(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k & =c(i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}t=c \\\\ 2+{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ 1+{t}^{2}=c(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $t=c.$ Substituting $c=t$ into the second equality gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{3} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =1 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis satisfies also the equation $1+{t}^{2}=c(2t).$\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{\u2032}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =i+(2+{1}^{3})\\,j+(1+{1}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+2\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{\u2032}(t)$ are parallel at the point $P(1,3,2).$",
